10:33:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re not wrong. Your old Nokia was optimized for a different set of priorities, and for “survive being dropped,” it was often objectively better. Those phones typically had: - A small display recessed behind a raised plastic bezel - A thick polycarbonate shell that flexed and absorbed impact - A lightweight body, so less impact energy in a fall - Lots of empty space and structural thickness around the screen - A plastic screen window that could scratch but was unlikely to shatter A modern iPhone is almost the opposite: - The display is huge and often extends close to every edge - The cover glass is exposed and may be curved at the edges - The phone is thin, heavy, and rigid—more energy hits the ground in a drop - Glass backs are often used for wireless charging and premium appearance - There is little protective bezel surrounding the vulnerable glass So even though modern cover glass is chemically strengthened and far tougher than ordinary window glass, the overall design makes it easier to crack: an edge or corner impact can concentrate force into the glass and start a fracture. The old Nokia tradeoff was that its plastic lens probably accumulated scratches, became hazy over time, and would not have worked as well for a large, bright, high-resolution touchscreen. But it remained functional and did not look like a spiderweb after a fall—which many people reasonably value more. Manufacturers could make mainstream phones with tough plastic display covers, thick bumpers, raised bezels, and replaceable front windows. Rugged phones already do variations of this. They tend to be thicker, less sleek, and their plastic surfaces are more scratch-prone, so the mainstream market has largely chosen “thin, premium-feeling, clear, scratch-resistant” over “nearly indestructible.” A case and a replaceable plastic or tempered-glass screen protector are basically an attempt to restore some of the old Nokia philosophy without giving up the modern glass display.
