10:33:30 | INFO | 🤖 Target: If by “tougher” you mean **less likely to crack when dropped**, then yes: a plastic cover is generally tougher than glass. Your Nokia experience reflects a real material advantage, not just nostalgia. Glass is hard and rigid, but brittle. Plastic is softer and more ductile: it can flex, deform, and absorb energy without forming a crack. For drop survival, especially with a modestly recessed screen, plastic is the more forgiving material. The reason glass won is that manufacturers prioritize a different definition of durability: - **Glass:** hard, stays clear, resists scratches and wear, feels premium—but can crack. - **Plastic:** impact-tough, light, and flexible—but scratches, scuffs, and can become hazy. Those design details are not merely excuses, though—they substantially change how likely *any* screen material is to fail. A large edge-to-edge glass slab on a heavy, thin phone is almost an ideal setup for cracks. Put that same glass behind a raised bumper in a thick plastic body and it becomes much more drop-resistant. Conversely, a large exposed plastic display would not shatter, but could get visibly scratched and dented fairly quickly. Still, it is absolutely a market choice, not a physical necessity. Companies could make phones that behave more like old Nokias: plastic display windows, chunky shock-absorbing bodies, raised bezels, and easy-to-replace front covers. Rugged phones do. Mainstream makers chose thinness, visual clarity, scratch resistance, and a “premium” glass-and-metal feel instead. And consumers have mostly been trained to accept a case and screen protector as part of owning a phone—which conveniently shifts some of the durability problem away from the phone’s design.
