10:34:00 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Fair point. If the durability that matters to you is **surviving drops and remaining usable**, then plastic/composite screen covers are more durable overall in the meaningful real-world sense. They flex, absorb impact, and usually scuff rather than catastrophically crack. Glass wins mainly on **surface appearance over time**: it stays smoother and more scratch-free. But that is a narrower advantage than “durability,” and it does not help much when the phone’s screen is shattered. So the honest summary is: - **Plastic/composite:** tougher, safer in drops, less likely to fail suddenly; may scratch or haze. - **Glass:** harder and more scratch-resistant; more brittle and prone to catastrophic cracking. Mainstream phones use glass not because it is the most rugged choice, but because manufacturers and much of the premium-phone market have treated scratch resistance, thinness, optical perfection, and a glass/metal feel as more desirable than maximum drop survivability.
