10:34:21 | INFO | 🤖 Target: They are not simply being stubborn—but they *are* making a deliberate tradeoff that may not match your priorities. Plastic is generally **tougher against impacts**: it flexes and is much less likely to crack. If the main goal is “drop it repeatedly and keep using it,” a plastic/composite outer screen can be the better choice. But it is not straightforwardly more durable in every way. An unprotected plastic screen can accumulate scratches, dents, coating wear, and optical haze much faster than hardened glass. Since a touchscreen is also the thing you look through and rub constantly, that matters to many buyers. So manufacturers are choosing: - **Glass:** better long-term surface clarity and scratch resistance; worse at severe impact. - **Plastic/composite:** better drop survival; worse surface wear unless augmented with costly hard coatings or replaceable layers. They could absolutely build mainstream phones around the latter approach—thicker bumpers, recessed screen edges, hard-coated polymer cover lenses, perhaps easily replaceable top layers. Rugged-phone makers already do versions of that. It would not be technologically impossible. The reason it is uncommon in flagship phones is that the market rewards thin, flat, glossy, scratch-resistant-looking devices, while cases and screen protectors partially mask the drop-risk problem. That is a product and marketing decision, not proof that glass is inherently the “better” material.
