10:34:39 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a perfectly reasonable priority. A phone that gets cosmetic scuffs but keeps working is, for many people, more durable in the way that actually matters. The counterargument is not literally “a tiny scratch.” On a large modern touchscreen, softer plastic can develop lots of fine scratches, dents, glare, and worn coatings. Over time that can make the display harder to see outdoors and less pleasant to use. But yes: those are usually gradual, non-catastrophic problems, whereas a glass crack can be sudden and expensive. So it is really a choice between two failure styles: - **Hard glass:** tends to look excellent until an unlucky impact causes a crack or shatter. - **Tough plastic/composite:** tends to survive impacts, but may slowly look worn from abrasion. Many consumers—including you—would reasonably choose the second. Manufacturers have largely optimized flagship phones for thinness, clarity, scratch-free showroom appearance, and a premium glass feel, while assuming buyers will use cases and screen protectors. That does not mean it is the universally sensible engineering choice; it means they have selected a different set of priorities. A better mainstream design could plausibly use a tough composite cover with a replaceable hard outer film, a modest raised bezel, and a shock-absorbing frame. It might acquire wear, but it would be much less likely to turn one ordinary drop into a broken phone.
