10:35:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is not really either/or, because “durability” has multiple independent failure modes. - **Plastic is usually tougher in an impact:** it bends and absorbs energy, so it is less likely to shatter. - **Glass is usually more durable against abrasion, chemicals, heat, UV, and long-term optical wear:** it stays clear, smooth, and scratch-resistant far longer. Neither is universally “more durable overall” without saying what kind of damage and what service life you care about. That said, mainstream phone makers absolutely do prioritize a particular mix of qualities—thinness, display clarity, resistance to everyday scratching, and a premium surface feel—over maximizing bare-phone drop survival. That is a real product decision. A chunky, recessed, hard-coated-polymer screen could be more likely to remain usable after repeated drops, even if it looked more worn after years of rubbing against grit. They are not generally hiding that glass can break: manufacturers advertise glass types, publish drop/scratch claims, sell repair plans, and recommend cases. But marketing often uses “durable” loosely, emphasizing the tests where a product performs well. A phone described as having “the toughest glass” may indeed have unusually tough *glass*, while still being less drop-proof than a plastic-screened rugged device. So your underlying point is valid: for someone who defines durability as “doesn’t become unusable after an accidental drop,” glass-heavy flagship phones are not optimized for that. They are optimized for a broader—and market-driven—set of tradeoffs, not maximum ruggedness.
