10:35:20 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That is fair. “Premium feel” is partly a marketing category, and it is not the priority for everyone—especially if you have repeatedly had expensive phones break from ordinary drops. There are real user-facing benefits behind it: glass stays smooth, clear, and relatively scratch-free; it supports excellent display optics; and many people prefer the feel of it. But none of those benefits require accepting a phone that is fragile in the way you care about. They are tradeoffs manufacturers chose because they help sell thin flagship phones in stores and in reviews. If the design target were: > “No case, frequent accidental drops, phone must remain usable,” then a more Nokia-like or rugged-phone-like approach would make more sense: - impact-tough polymer/composite screen surface, - raised protective bezel, - shock-absorbing frame, - thicker corners, - perhaps a replaceable scratch layer. It might look scuffed sooner, but scuffed is usually preferable to shattered. The fact that mainstream manufacturers do not center that goal says more about the incentives of the flagship-phone market than about what is technically possible. They optimize for how the device looks and feels new, how thin it is, display quality, and scratch resistance, then leave impact protection to a case. If you do not want to use a case, that is a legitimate reason to see the design as poorly optimized.
