10:35:56 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Exactly: phones are not different in the basic impact-physics sense. Safety goggles and many helmet visors use **polycarbonate** or similar tough plastics because, under impact, they deform and absorb energy instead of cracking into shards. That is why they are appropriate when the overriding requirement is “do not fail catastrophically when struck.” A phone screen made from a tough transparent polymer would get the same basic benefit: - it would be much less likely to crack from a drop, - it could flex slightly under impact, - it would be less likely to leave sharp broken edges, - and damage would more often be scuffs, dents, or abrasion rather than a spiderweb fracture. The reason phones often use glass is not that impact mechanics somehow reverse for phones. It is that phones impose another demanding requirement: the front surface is also a very thin, highly transparent, permanently exposed touch interface that people expect to remain pristine after years of rubbing against dust and grit. Polycarbonate visors and safety lenses are tough, but they scratch relatively easily; they often rely on hard coatings, and in many applications are treated as replaceable wear items. That is a solvable product-design issue, though. A phone maker could use, for example: - a tough polycarbonate/composite cover, - a hard scratch-resistant coating or sacrificial replaceable film, - a raised bezel and impact-absorbing frame. It would likely survive drops better than an exposed glass-front flagship. The cost would be more visible long-term surface wear, perhaps some optical/coating compromises, and possibly a thicker device. So your comparison is sound: safety equipment demonstrates that transparent plastic can be the better choice when impact survival matters most. Mainstream phone makers have simply put more weight on scratch-free appearance, extreme thinness, and display presentation than on making the bare phone survive repeated drops.
