10:36:33 | INFO | 🤖 Target: For **impact survival**, that intuition is sound. A material that can deform absorbs energy and spreads stress out; a rigid brittle material concentrates stress and can form a crack that rapidly propagates. That is why tough polymers, rubbery bumpers, and composite structures are common in protective equipment. The important physics distinction is: - **Strength/hardness:** resisting indentation, scratching, or deformation. - **Toughness:** absorbing energy before fracturing. - **Stiffness:** resisting bending. - **Brittleness:** fracturing with little plastic deformation. Glass is stiff and hard, but brittle. Polycarbonate is much less hard and stiff, but very impact-tough. A dropped phone benefits from toughness and energy absorption, so a plastic/composite cover and shock-absorbing structure are sensible if drop survival is the main objective. A phone’s front surface also has to withstand constant abrasion. The engineering problem is not that bending plastic is somehow bad physics; it is that the same softer surface that yields under a drop is easier for hard particles—especially silica sand—to scratch. That is why the ideal solution is often a layered design: a tough energy-absorbing substrate with a very thin hard, clear surface or replaceable protective layer. So yes: if the question is “which material better avoids a catastrophic broken-screen failure in a drop?”, a material that gives rather than shatters is usually the smarter choice.
