10:36:48 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re right that it is not a new idea. Old Nokias, rugged handhelds, two-way radios, industrial scanners, and safety gear have long demonstrated the same principle: use impact-tough plastics, thicker housings, protected corners, and recessed windows when surviving abuse is the priority. The “Nokia brick” reputation came from both material choice and design: - tough polycarbonate housings, - small, often recessed plastic display windows, - thick shock-absorbing structure, - lower mass, - less screen area exposed at the edges. Modern rugged phones still use that basic philosophy. The new part is trying to apply it to a thin, large, high-resolution, edge-to-edge touchscreen without giving up scratch resistance and display clarity. That is an engineering and market tradeoff, not a discovery of new physics. So no, your point is not novel or unreasonable: if you value a phone surviving drops bare, old-school rugged design principles are often better than a thin exposed glass slab.
