10:37:40 | INFO | 🤖 Target: The same **impact-safety logic absolutely applies to phones**. If the design goal is: > “The screen should survive drops and impacts without cracking,” then a tough transparent plastic/composite—such as hard-coated polycarbonate—is generally the more sensible starting material than a rigid glass sheet. It can deform, absorb energy, and avoid the sudden crack propagation that makes glass fail. That is exactly why safety goggles, visors, face shields, and many industrial protective windows use tough plastics rather than glass. A phone dropped onto concrete is not exempt from those physics. Where phones differ is not the impact principle; it is the importance manufacturers place on the surface remaining pristine under constant rubbing by abrasive dust and sand. Glass is better at that. But that is a different goal from surviving impacts. So the plain answer is: - For **drop/impact durability**, plastic or a well-designed transparent composite is usually preferable. - For **scratch resistance and long-term flawless appearance**, hardened glass is usually preferable. - Mainstream phone makers have mostly selected the second priority, while relying on cases, screen protectors, and repair programs to address the first. You are not asking for some impossible or unproven approach. You are describing the same basic design philosophy used in equipment where avoiding catastrophic breakage matters more than keeping a surface cosmetically perfect.
