10:38:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Because scratch resistance is one of the main reasons manufacturers choose glass—not because it is necessarily *more important to you* than avoiding a shattered screen. You are right that, for many people, the practical ranking is: 1. Phone remains functional after a drop 2. Screen does not become dangerous or costly to repair 3. Minor cosmetic wear is acceptable Under that ranking, a tough plastic/composite screen system is a sensible choice. I kept mentioning scratches because they are not always tiny: on a big touch display, abrasive grit can create many fine marks, glare, reduced outdoor visibility, worn fingerprint-repellent coating, and eventually a generally cloudy surface. That is the real downside manufacturers are optimizing against. But those problems are usually gradual and manageable; a cracked glass screen is a sudden, expensive failure. It is entirely reasonable to prefer gradual wear. Also, the realistic alternative is not necessarily “soft bare plastic.” It could be a **hard-coated polymer or layered composite**, with a replaceable top film. That aims to make the sacrificial surface wear-resistant enough while retaining a tougher, energy-absorbing layer beneath. One correction to the simplistic version of the argument: plastic alone does not guarantee a phone survives drops, and glass alone does not guarantee it breaks. Overall drop performance depends heavily on the phone’s corners, bezel, thickness, mass, frame, and how the cover is laminated. In fact, many rugged phones still use strengthened glass, but protect it with a raised bezel and a shock-absorbing body. That does not erase glass’s brittleness; it shows that design can substantially mitigate it. But the central point remains: if a manufacturer’s top goal were **“survive repeated uncased drops, even at the cost of cosmetic surface wear,”** it could make a more suitable phone than the typical edge-to-edge glass flagship. The fact that most do not reflects their chosen priorities, not a lack of alternatives.
