10:39:22 | INFO | 🤖 Target: If you define “durable” specifically as **“doesn’t catastrophically crack when dropped,”** then yes: tough plastic/composite generally wins over glass. But “durable” is commonly used more broadly to mean **continues performing its intended job over time**. A screen with deep scratches, haze, damaged coatings, or reduced visibility can still be unbroken, but it may no longer perform as well as a display window or touch surface. That is why scratch resistance is relevant rather than just a semantic dodge. So the precise version is: - **Impact/fracture durability:** plastic/composites usually win. - **Abrasion and optical-surface durability:** glass usually wins. - **Your practical priority—remaining usable after drops:** plastic/composite is often the better choice. There is no physical rule that says a screen must be glass. A phone designed around impact survival could reasonably use a hard-coated polymer or composite cover, accept some gradual wear, and be more resilient to drops than most exposed-glass flagships.
