10:39:42 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That concern is understandable. Cracked-screen repairs are expensive, disruptive, and common enough that “just use a case” can feel like manufacturers have normalized a design flaw. But the pressure for change does not require everyone to declare glass universally fine or plastic universally superior. It comes from demanding measurable outcomes, such as: - a specified number of drops onto concrete from a stated height, - no loss of display or touch function after those drops, - affordable screen replacement, - a raised protective bezel, - a replaceable outer wear layer, - independent impact testing—not just “our toughest glass.” A hard-coated polymer or composite front could be part of that solution, especially for phones marketed around uncased drop survival. So could better phone geometry and shock isolation. The important demand is not “use material X no matter what,” but: > Make the phone reliably survive ordinary drops, and publish meaningful evidence. That avoids letting “tougher glass” marketing substitute for real-world robustness. If consumers reward rugged designs, repairable screens, and clear drop-test claims, manufacturers have a reason to make those tradeoffs differently.
