10:40:14 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Phone screens are not an exception to plastic being impact-tough. Tough transparent plastics can indeed make a screen much less likely to shatter. The reason the comparison changes is that a phone screen is not just a protective shell: it is also an always-visible optical surface. It is rubbed thousands of times, exposed to abrasive mineral particles, skin oils, sunscreen, cleaners, heat, and sunlight, and expected to remain optically clear enough for a high-resolution display. A plastic cover can survive impacts while gradually becoming scratched, dented, or hazy. That is still a durability limitation, even if it is less catastrophic than cracking. So the straightforward position is: - If “durable” means **resists drops and stays usable rather than shattering**, plastic/composites are often the better choice. - If it means **resists long-term surface wear and remains optically pristine**, hardened glass is often the better choice. A phone company could reasonably choose plastic/composite and prioritize impact survival. That would not violate any physics or require an invented technology; it is a product tradeoff. Many rugged products already make it.
