10:40:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It may be part of why they last longer, but it is not enough evidence to say the plastic screen is *the* cause. Rugged phones that last a long time usually combine several things: - thicker, shock-absorbing housings and reinforced corners, - recessed screens or raised bezels, - stronger internal mounting for the battery, cameras, and circuit boards, - sealed ports and better dust/water protection, - lower-risk screen geometry, - sometimes user-replaceable batteries or parts, - and, in some models, tough plastic/composite outer layers or replaceable protectors. That whole system improves survival. The plastic cover helps specifically by reducing screen fracture risk, but a regular thin phone with only its glass swapped for plastic would still be vulnerable to frame bending, broken cameras, damaged connectors, battery damage, and corner impacts. Also, not all rugged phones use plastic cover lenses. Many use hardened glass—often Gorilla Glass—because of scratch resistance, then protect it with a raised bezel and an impact-absorbing body. That is evidence that the best rugged design is often about the whole structure, not one universally superior screen material. So your observation is consistent with plastic being beneficial for drop resilience. It just does not establish that plastic alone explains the longer lifespan.
