10:40:44 | INFO | 🤖 Target: On the narrow point of **resistance to cracking from impacts**, plastic is generally more durable than glass. But for the screen material itself, “more durable, full stop” still is not technically correct because the material also has to resist wear while remaining a clear touch surface: - **Plastic:** more impact-tough; less likely to crack or shatter. - **Glass:** much harder; less likely to scratch, wear hazy, or lose optical quality. That is not a phone-specific exception or marketing trick; it is a basic materials tradeoff. Polycarbonate safety lenses are tough, but they commonly need hard coatings and can still scratch more readily than glass. The same would apply to a phone. So the direct conclusion is: **if avoiding a broken screen after drops is the defining criterion, plastic/composite is the better screen-cover material.** If the criterion includes years of abrasion resistance and visual clarity, neither material wins outright.
